.TH HSH 1 "January 2026" "Holberton School" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hsh \- simple UNIX command interpreter

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ./hsh
.RI [ command\ ... ]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hsh
is a simple UNIX command interpreter developed as part of the Holberton School curriculum.
It reads commands from standard input, parses them into tokens, resolves executable paths,
and executes programs using
.BR fork (2)
and
.BR execve (2).

The shell supports both interactive and non-interactive modes.

.TP
.B Interactive mode
When standard input is connected to a terminal, the shell displays a prompt and waits for user commands.
.TP
.B Non-interactive mode
When input is provided via a pipe or file, commands are read and executed without displaying a prompt.

.SH INVOCATION
To start the shell, run:
.IP
.B ./hsh
.PP
To exit the shell, use the builtin command:
.IP
.B exit
.PP
or send an EOF signal (Ctrl-D).

.SH BUILTINS
Built-in commands are executed internally without searching the PATH.

.TP
.B exit
Terminates the shell. An optional numeric argument may be used as the exit status.
.TP
.B env
Prints the current environment variables.

.SH COMMAND EXECUTION
When a command is entered:
.IP \(bu 2
If the command matches a builtin, it is executed directly.
.IP \(bu 2
If the command contains a slash (\fB/\fP), it is treated as a path to an executable.
.IP \(bu 2
Otherwise, the shell searches for the command in the directories listed in the
.B PATH
environment variable.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B hsh
inherits the environment of the parent process.

Common variables include:
.TP
.B PATH
A colon-separated list of directories used to locate executable files.
.TP
.B HOME
The user home directory (not required, but commonly present).

.SH EXIT STATUS
The exit status of
.B hsh
is generally:
.IP \(bu 2
the exit status of the last executed command, or
.IP \(bu 2
a shell-defined value in case of an error.

If a command is not found, an error message is printed and the shell continues running
in interactive mode.

.SH SIGNALS
Signal handling depends on the implementation. Typically:
.IP \(bu 2
.B SIGINT
(Ctrl-C) is handled so that it interrupts the current command without exiting the shell.
.IP \(bu 2
Child processes receive signals normally.

.SH LIMITATIONS
As a minimalist educational shell,
.B hsh
may not support:
.IP \(bu 2
advanced quoting or escaping,
.IP \(bu 2
wildcard expansion (globbing),
.IP \(bu 2
command chaining (\fB;\fP),
.IP \(bu 2
logical operators (\fB&&\fP, \fB||\fP),
.IP \(bu 2
pipes or redirections.

.SH EXAMPLES
Interactive mode:
.IP
.EX
$ ./hsh
(hsh)$ ls -l
(hsh)$ env
(hsh)$ exit
.EE

Non-interactive mode:
.IP
.EX
$ echo "pwd" | ./hsh
/home/user
.EE

